  "id": "Compound::DB00188 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Bortezomib is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hematologic Cancer through a series of interconnected mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Bortezomib causes gastrointestinal ulcer perforation, which is caused by nilotinib, and nilotinib treats hematologic cancer. Similarly, Bortezomib causes corneal lesions, which are caused by bexarotene, and bexarotene treats hematologic cancer. Likewise, Bortezomib causes hemorrhagic stroke, which is caused by dasatinib, and dasatinib treats hematologic cancer. These relationships are mediated through the common ancestor of Bortezomib and the respective compounds, which are classified as diazines. The recurring theme is that Bortezomib, through its interaction with these compounds, contributes to the development of hematologic cancer. The evidence supports a potential role of Bortezomib in the pathogenesis of hematologic cancer via its interaction with compounds that treat this condition, suggesting a possible pathway involving the activation of signaling pathways or cellular processes that are targeted by these compounds. The integration of these pathways highlights the complex interplay between Bortezomib and other molecules that may influence the progression of hematologic cancer.",
